A||||3||Alex4mula||Injectors sizes pr HP requirements for 3.4. Check these formulas...||||1||
Z||000000||Alex4mula||04-19-2000||10:47 AM||||First of all I am not an expert in fuel injection and that is why I would like your feedback. All I know is what I read from magazines like the one I will be citing. I was reading this article on supercharging a 305 on CarCraft and they provided this interesting formula for calculating injector size per HP requirements. This is the formula:<P>(Injector lbs/hr) X (#Cyl )X (Duty cycle) X (two?) = HP rate<P>For example for the stock Fiero will be:<P>(15 lbs/hr) X (6cyl) X (0.8) X (2) = 144HP  which is about right at target.<P>They also have this formula that calculate rate (lbs/hr) increase for the injector when using a Fuel pressure regulator. It is like this:<P>SQRT(Increase pressure / Stock pressure) X (Stock Injector lbs/hr) = New Injector flow rate lbs/hr<P>Again, an example with 43.5psi stock pressure increased to 50psi and stock 15 lbs/hr injector will be:<P>SQRT(50psi/43.5psi) X (15 lbs/hr) = 16.08 lbs/hr<P>Using these formulas with different injector sizes and different fuel pressures you can reach to different HP ratings as seen in tables bellow. I will be installing (ordered already) a new 3.4 with FOCOA headers (+ K&N, pulleys, 8mm wires, etc)  into my Formula on May. I am doing all this because I am trying to decide if I use the stock 2.8 injectors with a fuel pressure regulator, or buy 19 lbs/hr injectors only, or buy the 19 lbs/hr injectors plus the FPR. By this numbers I think I will need the 19 lbs/hr with the FPR. What do you all think?? <P>HP rate by different injector size (no FPR):<BR>----------------------------------------------------<BR>lbs/hr	cyl	Duty cycle	two	HP Rate<BR>15	6	0.8		2	144<BR>17	6	0.8		2	163.2<BR>19	6	0.8		2	182.4<BR>24	6	0.8		2	230.4<P>HP rate by different injector size and FPR pressures (i.e. with FPR):<BR>-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------<BR>FPR	FPR 	lbs/hr	cyl	Duty	two	HP Rate<BR>Pressre	Factor			cycle<BR>50	1.07	15	6	0.8	2	154.4<BR>55	1.12	15	6	0.8	2	161.9<BR>60	1.17	15	6	0.8	2	169.1<BR>65	1.2	15	6	0.8	2	176.0<BR>70	1.27	15	6	0.8	2	182.7<BR>						<BR>50	1.07	19	6	0.8	2	195.6<BR>55	1.12	19	6	0.8	2	205.1<BR>60	1.17	19	6	0.8	2	214.2<BR>65	1.22	19	6	0.8	2	223.0<BR>70	1.27	19	6	0.8	2	231.4<BR>						<BR>50	1.07	24	6	0.8	2	247.0<BR>55	1.12	24	6	0.8	2	259.1<BR>60	1.17	24	6	0.8	2	270.6<BR>65	1.22	24	6	0.8	2	281.6<BR>70	1.27	24	6	0.8	2	292.3<P><BR>Another think to consider is if using 19 lbs/hr injectors will cause it to run rich most of the time (computer may not be able to compensate) and at smog test they could screw the marginal test numbers I am getting now. Expertise, experience and just plain jokes are welcome. Thanks.<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||80's BOY||04-19-2000||01:46 PM||||way to go! i used some formulas to figure out proper flow rate for my 3.1 stroker 6cyl. i came up with about 18.2 lbs per hour using this formula....<P>          esimated hp x BSFC     <BR>          ------------------     = flow rate<BR>          no. of injectors x 0.8 <P> <BR>BSFC means Brake Specific Fuel Consumption. this number varies between 0.40-0.60 for gas engines. i used 0.50 as a good medium for high performance applications. a more accurate test would be to dyno your car, then get the hp number instead of estimating like i did. my stroker should be around 185-190 hp HOPEFULLY. i have not dyno'd it yet, but from my tests, it IS running lean. i have purchased a set of firebird injectors to replace the 2.8 ones with, but have not installed them yet. a yes or no answer as to whether or not i need a pressure regulator or what type on my stroked 2.8, now 3.1 has not really been justified. so i am asking for help on the same topic. i am not fond of the factory regulator position, so an in-line regulator would seem more practical to me. maybe other people have more experience than me on regulators and where to put them. the firebird injectors are supposedly from the factory at 18.5 lbs per hr and a set of these are reasonably less expensive than a new set of high performance injectors that have around the same results. so far, there is no promise that any of this will make any difference without changing your PROM to compensate for the mods. any help or input is appreciated.||||reg||3
Z||000002||Alex4mula||04-20-2000||08:37 AM||||Well... I guess I put too many formulas or wrote too much. Anybody with a 3.4 installed out there? What injectors are you using? Thanks again.||||reg||9
